A minimal worker-side pattern is:

```js
// worker.js
self.onmessage = async ({ data: { id, url } }) => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);

    const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(await response.blob());
    self.postMessage({ id, bitmap }, [bitmap]);
  } catch (error) {
    self.postMessage({ id, error: String(error) });
  }
};
```

```js
// main thread
worker.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  if (data.error) {
    console.error(`Frame ${data.id}: ${data.error}`);
    return;
  }

  ctx.drawImage(data.bitmap, 0, 0);
  data.bitmap.close(); // if this frame is no longer needed
};
```

The transfer list matters: it transfers ownership of the bitmap to the receiver. Add queue limits, frame IDs for stale requests, and error handling in production. [Chrome’s worker example](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/createimagebitmap-in-chrome-50) · [MDN: transferable objects](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Transferable_objects)

