Verified the change in the built app and left it uncommitted for your review.

**What the research says actually moves the needle** (the full report came back with sources):

- Don't block entry on every frame — the biggest perceived win ([web.dev preload guidance](https://web.dev/articles/preload-responsive-images), same advice from every sequence-performance write-up).
- Serve frames near their rendered size; fewer pixels ≈ proportional byte cut ([MDN responsive images](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Guides/Responsive_images)).
- AVIF is ~20–25 % smaller than WebP at equal quality, but decode costs more ([Crystallize](https://crystallize.com/blog/avif-vs-webp)); JPEG XL isn't safe as a sole format yet ([caniuse](https://caniuse.com/jpegxl)).
- Replacing the sequence with video is 5–10× smaller but `video.currentTime` is **not** frame-accurate, so scrubbing/`playTo(frame)` breaks ([W3C](https://www.w3.org/2019/Talks/TPAC/frame-accurate-sync/), [web.dev RVFC](https://web.dev/articles/requestvideoframecallback-rvfc)).
- HTTP/3 and sprite sheets are noise here: an atlas saved only ~10 % in a measured case ([geyer.dev](https://geyer.dev/blog/css-image-sequence-animations/)), and protocol choice doesn't shrink 8 MB.
- Don't use Vercel's image optimizer for 177 frames — it bills per transformation ([pricing](https://vercel.com/docs/image-optimization/limits-and-pricing)).

**Two real defects that were behind the wait**

1. The entry gate waited for the *entire* wheel clip (81 frames) as well as the keypad, even though the wheel isn't drawn until after the entrance + keypad demo + its own `bank()` guard. It now waits only for the keypad up to K1 (49 frames) and lets the wheel stream underneath; on a phone (no keypad set) it still waits for the whole wheel.
2. The clip worker had no failure path: if the worker module didn't start, the first-frame promise never settled, so the gate sat on zero frames until its 25 s timeout. The player now waits for a `ready` handshake with a 5 s budget plus `onerror`, and falls back to the main-thread loader — which also had to be taught to decode as frames arrive, otherwise the fallback could never satisfy the gate either.

**Measured** (production build, Chromium, CDP network emulation, fresh profile each run):

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| gate release @ 10 Mbps | 8.5 s | 4.9–5.4 s |
| curtain gone @ 10 Mbps | 11.0 s | 6.2 s |
| gate release, unthrottled | 4.6 s | 2.9 s |
| simulated worker failure | never opens (25 s timeout) | opens at 3.4 s via fallback |

Changed: [HeroButtons.tsx](src/components/HeroButtons.tsx) (gate criterion + keypad-first download order), [clipPlayer.ts](src/lib/clipPlayer.ts) (worker handshake, timeout, `onerror`, decoding inline fallback), [clipWorker.ts](src/lib/clipWorker.ts) (posts `ready` on init).

Also note: `npx playwright test` gives 8 failures, all pre-existing and unrelated — they target the old landing (`[data-testid="vault-sequence"] .vs-cta`, "Put it to Test") that no longer exists in `src/`.

Still on the table if you want more: AVIF variants would cut roughly another 45 % of the gate's bytes (≈1 s on a 10 Mbps link), at the cost of 177 extra files and a format fallback.
