**Double-press.** Touch had a deliberate "first tap arms, second tap leaves" path: `touchstart` called `preventDefault()`, which swallowed the click, then the click that finally landed played the 3-second wheel turn before navigating. That path is gone. A tap is now the action, and the signup/signin transition is the motion. Measured on an iPhone-sized, touch-only context: one tap → `/signup` in **39 ms**, one tap → `/signin` in **67 ms**. On a pointer device nothing changed — hover still previews the turn (clip frame 16 after 1.2 s of hover) and the click lands on it.

**The black line.** The canvases are feathered into the page with `mask-image`, but only the **unprefixed** property was written. Any browser that hasn't shipped it — Chrome before 120, older iOS, most in-app browsers — dropped the fade, so the frame's own shadow under the safe was cut dead straight where the vault band ends. That's the line your arrow points at.

Verified the diagnosis by toggling the feather off in a phone viewport and scanning the band's rows: with the feather the rows at the cut read a uniform 204–212; without it they ramp 208 → 173 and then jump to 212 exactly at the band's bottom edge — a 39-level step, i.e. a visible dark line. The prefixed property is now written alongside both gradients (base and phone override), so the fade applies either way. Confirmed in the deployed CSS.

