<h1>Vaultpay AI: Backend Handoff</h1>
<p class="meta">For the backend team. Source: the frontend repository as of October 30, 2026.</p>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Stack correction.</strong> The frontend is a Vite + React 18 + React Router single-page app. It is not Next.js.
  Nothing in the repository uses Supabase, PayPal, Gemini or Azure yet. Every backend behavior is simulated in the
  browser: <code>src/lib/api.ts</code> holds module-level arrays, and <code>src/lib/store.tsx</code> and
  <code>src/lib/mockAuth.ts</code> hold the rest. The backend must be built from scratch.
  The team needs to decide whether to add API routes to a Next.js app (the route handlers in section 3 assume this)
  or to keep Vite and run a separate Node or Edge API. Either way, the JSON contracts in section 3 stay the same.
</div>

<h2>1. Current state</h2>

<h3>1.1 What is built</h3>
<p>The app has four surfaces: a public landing and auth flow, a vault room where an audit runs, a client portal for
delivering files, and a client dashboard. The product flow is an escrow-style payment. A client authorizes funds for a
freelancer's project, the funds are held, an audit runs, and the client approves (capture) or rejects (void). Approval
unlocks the delivery files for the freelancer.</p>

<h3>1.2 Routing (<code>src/App.tsx</code>)</h3>
<p>Routes are defined with <code>createBrowserRouter</code>. The vault layout mounts once and wraps the public pages.</p>
